   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let F be the antiderivative of \\( f(x) = e^{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\) with \\( F(0) = 1 \\). Find the exact value of \\( F\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": "ExponentialE"
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Cos",
            "x"
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Then \\( du = - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\, dx \\), so \\( \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\, dx = -du \\).",
         "mathjson": -1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Since \\( F(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) = F(0) + \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} e^{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\, dx \\), convert the limits: \\( x = 0 \\) gives \\( u = 1 \\) and \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\) gives \\( u = 0 \\), so the integral becomes \\( \\int_{1}^{0} - e^{u} \\, du \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Exp",
               "u"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "An antiderivative of \\( - e^{u} \\) is \\( - e^{u} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Exp",
               "u"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 5,
         "text": "The integral is \\( -1 - \\left(- e\\right) = -1 + e \\), so \\( F(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) = 1 + \\left(-1 + e\\right) = e \\).",
         "mathjson": "ExponentialE"
      }
   ],
